<edge source="us_trade_unilateralism::dispute_intensity_index" target="global_energy_reorder::policy_intervention_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating unilateral trade disputes prompt affected governments and trading partners to respond with more frequent and larger energy-sector interventions (subsidies, export controls, state procurement deals) to protect supply chains and strategic industries.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_unilateralism</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_reorder</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_unilateralism::dispute_intensity_index" target="russia_ukraine_war::allied_alignment_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating unilateral US trade disputes with allied economies (tariffs, threats, retaliatory steps and high-profile rhetoric) erode trust and reciprocity, reducing partners' willingness to coordinate policy and military support for Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_unilateralism</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_unilateralism::dispute_intensity_index" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_feedback" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">More intense unilateral US trade disputes generate reciprocal economic and rhetorical responses from Russia and its partners, strengthening feedback loops that convert each punitive action into amplified reciprocal measures within the conflict system.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_unilateralism</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_unilateralism::dispute_intensity_index" target="us_bond_market_tension::portfolio_flows" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Heightened bilateral trade dispute intensity raises global risk and policy uncertainty, prompting a near-term flight-to-safety that reallocates cross-asset portfolios into U.S. Treasuries and other safe assets, increasing net portfolio inflows into bonds.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_unilateralism</data>
  <data key="d39">us_bond_market_tension</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_unilateralism::dispute_intensity_index" target="us_iran_conflict::strait_interference_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Marked escalation in US trade-dispute measures and adversarial rhetoric raises bilateral political tensions and motivates Iranian state or proxy forces to harass or interdict commercial traffic in the strait as a retaliatory or leverage tactic.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_unilateralism</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_unilateralism::dispute_intensity_index" target="us_iran_conflict::insurance_premium_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in trade-dispute intensity increases the perceived likelihood of retaliatory maritime incidents near Iran, leading insurers to raise route- and war-risk premiums for voyages through or near the strait.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_unilateralism</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_conflict</data>
</edge>
